# Insights & results — decisions

## Information architecture
**Chose:** Nest Overview, Openings, Strategy, and Endgames under `/results/$username`; keep one top-level Results link and redirect legacy routes.
**Why:** ⚠️ NEEDS INPUT — routes and `ResultsSectionNav` establish this structure, but no rationale was found.

## Accuracy aggregation
**Chose:** Use RMS accuracy for game, strategy, and endgame aggregates; use ACPL fallback where only legacy opening aggregates exist.
**Considered:** Arithmetic mean for move accuracy.
**Why:** `averageAccuracy` says arithmetic mean reads about 5–15 points high versus Chess.com (source: `src/lib/analysis/classify.ts`). Opening fallback is labeled for legacy ACPL-only aggregates.

## Peer comparisons
**Chose:** Use real SQL cohort RPCs for Strategy and Endgames and render `—` when the RPC returns null.
**Considered:** The synthetic `reportStats.peerPercentile` formula used by Review.
**Why:** The migration enforces minimum samples and `InsightStats` preserves null as `—`; no opening peer RPC exists. The particular sample thresholds are ⚠️ NEEDS INPUT.

## Opening peer gap
**Chose:** Omit similar-rating from openings. Strategy and Endgames keep the cohort column because those RPCs exist.
**Considered:** An empty `—` column as a placeholder.
**Why:** A blank peer column reads as missing data, not as an honest gap.

## Opening repertoire color
**Chose:** Split most-played opening lists by White vs Black. Mixed-color lists hid which side the line was played from.
**Considered:** An “all games” combined list.
**Why:** Italian as White and Sicilian as Black are different repertoires. The control is a two-way swap, same as the trainer.

## Game-type normalization
**Chose:** Normalize filters into Overall, Bullet, Blitz, Rapid, Daily, and Other, and pass the same class to peer RPCs.
**Why:** ⚠️ NEEDS INPUT — implementation and tests establish the buckets, but no rationale was found.

## Letter grades
**Chose:** Convert accuracy and conversion percentages to fixed letter-grade cutoffs.
**Why:** ⚠️ NEEDS INPUT — `src/lib/grades.ts` defines the cutoffs without explaining them.

## Overview playstyle
**Chose:** Derive Overview playstyle from stored `strategy_stats` (open / closed / semi-closed RMS accuracy) plus color, clock, motif kind, and time class. Headline is relative accuracy of the best sampled center type versus the worst (≥40 moves, ≥3% gap). Traits need a real split (2 pt error-rate, 3 pt accuracy, 60% motif share) and one-decimal labels. Hide the panel until a structure sample or trait exists.
**Considered:** A peer RPC or Review `peerPercentile`. Showing every 0.9-point or 57/43 split as style.
**Why:** Structure buckets already persist on games; inventing evals or explorer frequencies is forbidden; no opening peer RPC exists. Tiny gaps are float noise, not a playstyle.

## Chart outlier handling
**Chose:** Exclude unanalyzed or mate-inflated rows through `usableAccuracy` and `usableAcpl`.
**Considered:** Plotting those rows.
**Why:** `classify.ts` says a 0% / 5000-ACPL game would otherwise flatten the chart.
